            <div xml:id="d166">

                <pb facs="0481" n="453" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v17/pg453"/>

                <head>166. Telegram From the Department of State to the
                        Embassy in Romania<anchor n="1" xml:id="d166-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1968-08-31T21:47:00+00:00">August 31, 1968,
                        2147Z</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>231372. Subject: Bogdan-Leddy Conversation.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>In uninstructed call which, however, approved by Foreign Minister
                            Manescu, Romanian Ambassador
                            Bogdan inquired August 31 as to
                        nature any new evidence on Soviet intentions which may have triggered
                        Presidential statement August 30.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d166-f2"/>
                        Leddy replied that military-type
                        evidence remained inconclusive but that, on basis potentially ominous
                        request by Dobrynin for appointment
                        with Secretary for August 31 morning and similar requests by Soviets in
                        certain other capitals, Secretary had decided call in Dobrynin evening August 30.<anchor n="3"
                            xml:id="d166-f3"/> Our concern over possibility Soviets might intervene
                        in Romania had led to last-minute inclusion of Eastern European portion of
                        President’s speech.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>
                        Leddy briefly characterized Dobrynin oral statement defending Soviet
                        action in Czechoslovakia and read aloud pertinent last paragraph giving
                        first priority to defending socialism. (Deptel 231323)<anchor n="4" xml:id="d166-f4"/>
                    </item>
                    <pb facs="0482" n="454" xml:id="pg454"/>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>
                        Leddy informed Bogdan of general theme of Dobrynin’s communication and partial
                        substance Secretary’s remarks on Romania reported Deptel 231337.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d166-f5"/> Main points
                        mentioned by Leddy were: (a)
                        Secretary said various reports received during past 24 hours had been
                        disturbing. (b) Dobrynin replied he
                        had no information. (c) Secretary said if Soviets contemplated invasion of
                        Romania, we ask in name of humanity, that it not be done and that such
                        action would have incalculable effects on world situation. (d) Secretary
                        emphasized to Dobrynin none of
                        reports re possible invasion to which we had referred had come from
                        Romanians.</item>
                    <label>4.</label>
                    <item>
                        Bogdan appreciated info and said
                            Manescu had planned depart
                        Bucharest in order receive UK Foreign
                        Secretary Stewart in Bucharest next week but had decided remain New York
                        until situation clearer.</item>
                </list>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#bold">Rusk</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
